WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.460
A mixture of plant species can outyield its monocultures.

2
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.040
Does it do so for one reason, or for two?

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.260
Take a made-up example.

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.560
Two species are sown in equal proportions.

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.180
Species A alone yields 400 grams of dry biomass per square metre.

6
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.860
Species B alone yields 100.

7
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.780
Sown together, they yield 400.

8
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.940
Is that a gain, and where does it come from?

9
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.653
First, set a benchmark.

10
00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:38.713
The expected mixture yield is the mean of the two monocultures.

11
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:43.313
400 plus 100, over 2, is 250.

12
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:46.453
The observed yield is 400.

13
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:51.993
The net effect is the difference: plus 150 grams per square metre.

14
00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:54.373
That gain has two parts.

15
00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:57.747
Complementarity is the first part.

16
00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:04.007
On average, species do better in mixture than their monocultures predict, as they

17
00:01:04.007 --> 00:01:07.667
would if they used resources differently or helped one another.

18
00:01:08.947 --> 00:01:10.607
Selection is the second part.

19
00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:16.647
A random mix is more likely to include one highly productive species, and that

20
00:01:16.647 --> 00:01:17.647
species dominates.

21
00:01:18.587 --> 00:01:20.247
The partition measures both.

22
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.230
Now measure each species against its own monoculture.

23
00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.730
Species A yields 300 in the mixture, out of 400 alone.

24
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.170
Its relative yield is 0.75.

25
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.930
Species B yields 100, out of 100 alone.

26
00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.210
Its relative yield is 1.

27
00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.450
Each was sown at half, so each was expected at 0.5.

28
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.770
The gains are 0.25 and 0.5.

29
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:58.513
Complementarity is richness times the mean gain times the mean monoculture yield.

30
00:01:59.593 --> 00:02:07.273
Two times 0.375 times 250 is plus 187.5.

31
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:13.273
Selection asks whether the species with high monoculture yields gained most.

32
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.213
Here the larger gain went to species B, the low yielder.

33
00:02:18.213 --> 00:02:22.673
So selection is negative: minus 37.5.

34
00:02:23.853 --> 00:02:27.213
Add them, and you get 150, the net effect.

35
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:31.157
Last, compare with the best monoculture.

36
00:02:31.877 --> 00:02:37.057
The mixture yields 400, exactly what species A yields alone.

37
00:02:37.397 --> 00:02:40.257
So there is no transgressive overyielding.

38
00:02:40.957 --> 00:02:44.737
The mixture beats the average monoculture, but not the best.

39
00:02:45.597 --> 00:02:50.937
Also, a positive complementarity term does not prove resource partitioning.

40
00:02:51.457 --> 00:02:56.537
Keep this rule: the partition splits the gain, but it names no mechanism.
